## Purpose and Scope

The Workflow Execution Engine is the core orchestration system that executes compiled workflow definitions using Temporal. This page covers the main workflow function `shipsecWorkflowRun`, action scheduling and dependency resolution, signal/query handlers, and integration with Temporal activities. For workflow compilation details, see [Workflow Compilation](#2.3). For activity implementation details, see [Activity Execution](#8.2). For scheduler internals, see [Workflow Scheduler](#8.5).

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:1-987]()

---

## Execution Architecture Overview

The execution engine consists of three primary layers: the Temporal workflow function, the dependency scheduler, and activity executors. Workflows are executed within Temporal's durable execution environment, maintaining state across failures and long-running operations.

### System Components

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Backend Layer"
        TemporalService["TemporalService<br/>(backend)"]
        WorkflowsService["WorkflowsService"]
    end

    subgraph "Temporal Server"
        TaskQueue["Task Queue<br/>shipsec-default"]
        WorkflowExecution["Workflow Execution<br/>shipsecWorkflowRun"]
    end

    subgraph "Worker Layer"
        Worker["Temporal Worker<br/>dev.worker.ts"]
        WorkflowScheduler["WorkflowScheduler<br/>runWorkflowWithScheduler"]
        Activities["Activity Functions<br/>runComponentActivity<br/>prepareRunPayloadActivity<br/>recordTraceEventActivity"]
    end

    subgraph "Execution State"
        Results["Map<string, unknown><br/>Action Results"]
        PendingInputs["Map<nodeRef, resolution><br/>Human Inputs"]
        PendingTools["Map<callId, result><br/>Tool Calls"]
    end

    TemporalService -->|"startWorkflow()"| TaskQueue
    TaskQueue -->|"dispatch"| Worker
    Worker -->|"executes"| WorkflowExecution
    WorkflowExecution -->|"delegates to"| WorkflowScheduler
    WorkflowScheduler -->|"executes"| Activities

    WorkflowExecution -->|"maintains"| Results
    WorkflowExecution -->|"tracks"| PendingInputs
    WorkflowExecution -->|"tracks"| PendingTools

    WorkflowsService -->|"prepareRunPayload"| TemporalService
```

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:156-1032](), [backend/src/temporal/temporal.service.ts:88-451](), [worker/src/temporal/workers/dev.worker.ts:81-401]()

---

## The shipsecWorkflowRun Workflow

The `shipsecWorkflowRun` function is the main Temporal workflow that orchestrates all action execution. It receives a `RunWorkflowActivityInput` containing the compiled workflow definition, runtime inputs, and execution metadata.

### Workflow Signature

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `input.runId` | `string` | Unique identifier for this execution run |
| `input.workflowId` | `string` | ID of the workflow being executed |
| `input.definition` | `WorkflowDefinition` | Compiled workflow definition with actions and dependencies |
| `input.inputs` | `Record<string, unknown>` | Runtime inputs for the entrypoint |
| `input.depth` | `number` | Sub-workflow nesting depth (default: 0) |
| `input.callChain` | `string[]` | Chain of workflow IDs to detect cycles |

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:156-158](), [worker/src/temporal/types.ts:124-136]()

### Workflow State Management

The workflow maintains three key state maps:

```typescript
// Action results storage
const results = new Map<string, unknown>();

// Pending human input resolutions
const pendingHumanInputs = new Map<string, { nodeRef: string; resolve: (res: HumanInputResolution) => void }>();
const humanInputResolutions = new Map<string, HumanInputResolution>();

// Pending tool call results
const pendingToolCalls = new Map<string, { request: ToolCallRequest; resolve: (result: ToolCallResult) => void }>();
const toolCallResults = new Map<string, ToolCallResult>();
```

These maps enable the workflow to track execution progress, coordinate asynchronous operations, and resume after signal handlers fire.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:159-188]()

---

## Workflow Lifecycle

### Initialization Phase

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> SetMetadata: "Workflow started"
    SetMetadata --> BuildActionMap: "setRunMetadataActivity"
    BuildActionMap --> InitializeSignals: "Create actionsByRef Map"
    InitializeSignals --> StartScheduler: "Register signal handlers"
    StartScheduler --> ExecuteActions: "runWorkflowWithScheduler"

    ExecuteActions --> ActionLoop: "For each action"
    ActionLoop --> CheckEntrypoint: "Is entrypoint?"
    CheckEntrypoint --> ApplyInputs: "Yes: Apply runtime inputs"
    CheckEntrypoint --> BuildPayload: "No: Build from mappings"
    ApplyInputs --> ExecuteComponent
    BuildPayload --> ExecuteComponent

    ExecuteComponent --> StoreResult: "Activity completes"
    StoreResult --> ActionLoop: "More actions"
    ActionLoop --> Finalize: "All complete"
    Finalize --> [*]: "Return outputs"

    note right of SetMetadata
        Activity: setRunMetadataActivity
        Updates workflow_runs table
    end note

    note right of ExecuteComponent
        Activity: runComponentActivity
        Runs component with resolved inputs
    end note
```

The workflow begins by registering metadata with `setRunMetadataActivity`, building an action lookup map, and initializing signal handlers for human inputs and tool calls. It then delegates to `runWorkflowWithScheduler` for dependency-based execution.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:278-300](), [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:314-848]()

### Entrypoint Input Handling

The workflow applies runtime inputs only to the designated entrypoint component:

```typescript
const isEntrypointRef = input.definition.entrypoint.ref === action.ref;
const isEntrypointComponent = action.componentId === 'core.workflow.entrypoint';

if (isEntrypointRef && input.inputs) {
  if (isEntrypointComponent) {
    console.log(`[Workflow] Applying inputs to entrypoint component '${action.ref}' (${action.componentId})`);
    mergedInputs.__runtimeData = input.inputs;
  } else {
    console.error(`[Workflow] CRITICAL: Entrypoint ref '${input.definition.entrypoint.ref}' points to component '${action.componentId}' instead of 'core.workflow.entrypoint'.`);
  }
}
```

This ensures that only the correct entrypoint node receives the workflow's runtime inputs, avoiding accidental data leakage to other nodes.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:328-352]()

---

## Action Orchestration

### Workflow Scheduler Integration

The execution engine delegates action scheduling to `runWorkflowWithScheduler`, which handles dependency resolution, join strategies, and parallel execution:

```typescript
await runWorkflowWithScheduler(input.definition, {
  onNodeSkipped: async (actionRef) => {
    await recordTraceEventActivity({
      type: 'NODE_SKIPPED',
      runId: input.runId,
      nodeRef: actionRef,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      level: 'info',
    });
  },
  run: async (actionRef, schedulerContext) => {
    const action = actionsByRef.get(actionRef);
    // Execute action...
  }
});
```

The scheduler calls the `run` callback for each action when dependencies are satisfied, passing a `schedulerContext` containing metadata about which dependencies triggered the execution.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:300-314](), [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:314-848]()

### Action Execution Flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start["Scheduler calls run()"] --> GetAction["Get action from actionsByRef"]
    GetAction --> BuildPayload["buildActionPayload()<br/>Resolve inputs from results map"]
    BuildPayload --> CheckType{"Action type?"}

    CheckType -->|"core.workflow.call"| SubWorkflow["Sub-Workflow Handler"]
    CheckType -->|"tool mode"| ToolMode["Tool Registration"]
    CheckType -->|"normal"| NormalExec["Component Execution"]

    SubWorkflow --> PrepareChild["prepareRunPayloadActivity"]
    PrepareChild --> StartChild["startChild(shipsecWorkflowRun)"]
    StartChild --> WaitChild["Wait for result with timeout"]
    WaitChild --> StoreChildResult["Store child outputs"]

    ToolMode --> CheckMcp{"MCP server?"}
    CheckMcp -->|"Yes"| RunMcp["runComponentActivity<br/>Start container"]
    CheckMcp -->|"No"| RegisterTool["prepareAndRegisterToolActivity"]
    RunMcp --> RegisterLocalMcp["registerLocalMcpActivity"]
    RegisterTool --> MarkReady["Store tool result"]
    RegisterLocalMcp --> MarkReady

    NormalExec --> WaitTools{"Has tool dependencies?"}
    WaitTools -->|"Yes"| PollTools["areAllToolsReadyActivity<br/>Poll until ready"]
    WaitTools -->|"No"| RunComponent
    PollTools --> RunComponent["runComponentActivity"]
    RunComponent --> CheckPending{"Pending approval?"}

    CheckPending -->|"Yes"| CreateRequest["createHumanInputRequestActivity"]
    CreateRequest --> WaitSignal["Wait for resolveHumanInputSignal"]
    WaitSignal --> StoreApproval["Store approval result"]

    CheckPending -->|"No"| StoreResult["Store output in results map"]
    StoreApproval --> StoreResult
    StoreChildResult --> StoreResult
    MarkReady --> StoreResult

    StoreResult --> RecordComplete["recordTraceEventActivity<br/>NODE_COMPLETED"]
    RecordComplete --> End["Return activePorts"]
```

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:314-848]()

### Component Activity Execution

Regular component execution delegates to `runComponentActivity` with retry policies:

```typescript
const { runComponentActivity: runComponentWithRetry } = proxyActivities<{
  runComponentActivity(input: RunComponentActivityInput): Promise<RunComponentActivityOutput>;
}>({
  startToCloseTimeout: '10 minutes',
  retry: retryOptions,
});

const output = await runComponentWithRetry(activityInput);
```

The activity input includes resolved inputs, parameters, metadata about join strategy and triggering dependencies, and connected tool node IDs for components that need MCP integration.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:788-847](), [worker/src/temporal/types.ts:74-106]()

---

## Signal Handling

### Human Input Resolution Signal

The workflow uses the `resolveHumanInputSignal` to coordinate human-in-the-loop approvals:

```typescript
setHandler(resolveHumanInputSignal, (resolution: HumanInputResolution) => {
  console.log(`[Workflow] Received human input signal for ${resolution.nodeRef}: approved=${resolution.approved}`);
  humanInputResolutions.set(resolution.nodeRef, resolution);
  const pending = pendingHumanInputs.get(resolution.nodeRef);
  if (pending) {
    pending.resolve(resolution);
  }
});
```

When a component returns an approval-pending output, the workflow creates a request record, then waits using `condition()`:

```typescript
if (isApprovalPending(output.output)) {
  const approvalResult = await createHumanInputRequestActivity({
    runId: input.runId,
    workflowId: input.workflowId,
    nodeRef: action.ref,
    inputType: pendingData.inputType ?? 'approval',
    title: pendingData.title,
    timeoutMs: pendingData.timeoutAt ? new Date(pendingData.timeoutAt).getTime() - Date.now() : undefined,
  });

  let signalReceived = await condition(() => humanInputResolutions.has(action.ref), timeoutMs);

  if (!signalReceived) {
    await expireHumanInputRequestActivity(approvalResult.requestId);
    throw ApplicationFailure.nonRetryable('Human input request timed out');
  }

  const resolution = humanInputResolutions.get(action.ref)!;
  results.set(action.ref, { approved: resolution.approved, ... });
}
```

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:172-181](), [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:868-1000]()

### Tool Call Execution Signal

The `executeToolCallSignal` enables MCP agents to invoke components as tools:

```typescript
setHandler(executeToolCallSignal, async (request: ToolCallRequest) => {
  if (toolCallResults.has(request.callId)) {
    console.warn(`[Workflow] Duplicate tool call ignored: ${request.callId}`);
    return;
  }

  const TOOL_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS = 300000; // 5 minutes
  const activityOutput = await Promise.race([
    _runComponentActivity({
      runId: input.runId,
      action: { ref: `tool-call:${request.callId}`, componentId: request.componentId },
      inputs: { ...(request.credentials ?? {}), ...request.arguments },
      params: request.parameters ?? {},
      metadata: { streamId: request.callId },
    }),
    new Promise((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Tool call timed out')), TOOL_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS)),
  ]);

  const result: ToolCallResult = {
    callId: request.callId,
    success: true,
    output: activityOutput.output,
    completedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
  };

  toolCallResults.set(request.callId, result);
});
```

This signal executes synchronously, running the component activity and storing the result. The MCP Gateway queries for results using the `getToolCallResult` query handler.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:191-268]()

---

## Query Handlers

### Tool Call Result Query

The workflow exposes a query handler for retrieving tool call results:

```typescript
setHandler(
  defineQuery<ToolCallResult | null, [string]>('getToolCallResult'),
  (callId: string) => {
    return toolCallResults.get(callId) ?? null;
  }
);
```

This enables the MCP Gateway to poll for tool call completion without blocking on the signal, implementing the signal-and-poll pattern for external tool execution.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:271-276]()

---

## Sub-Workflow Execution

### Depth and Cycle Protection

Sub-workflow calls have built-in protections against infinite recursion:

```typescript
const MAX_SUBWORKFLOW_DEPTH = 10;

if (depth >= MAX_SUBWORKFLOW_DEPTH) {
  throw ApplicationFailure.nonRetryable(
    `Maximum sub-workflow nesting depth (${MAX_SUBWORKFLOW_DEPTH}) exceeded`,
    'SubWorkflowDepthError'
  );
}

const callChain = Array.isArray(input.callChain) && input.callChain.length > 0
  ? input.callChain
  : [input.workflowId];

if (callChain.includes(childWorkflowId)) {
  throw ApplicationFailure.nonRetryable(
    `Circular sub-workflow call detected for workflow ${childWorkflowId}`,
    'SubWorkflowCycleError',
    [{ callChain }]
  );
}
```

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:355-404]()

### Child Workflow Lifecycle

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Parent as "Parent Workflow"
    participant Activity as "prepareRunPayloadActivity"
    participant Temporal as "Temporal Server"
    participant Child as "Child Workflow"

    Parent->>Activity: "Prepare child run payload"
    Activity-->>Parent: "PreparedRunPayload"

    Parent->>Temporal: "startChild(shipsecWorkflowRun)"
    Temporal->>Child: "Start execution"

    Parent->>Parent: "Race: child.result() vs sleep(timeoutMs)"

    alt Child completes successfully
        Child-->>Parent: "{ success: true, outputs }"
        Parent->>Parent: "Store outputs in results map"
        Parent->>Activity: "recordTraceEventActivity(NODE_COMPLETED)"
    else Child fails
        Child-->>Parent: "Throw error"
        Parent->>Activity: "recordTraceEventActivity(NODE_FAILED)"
        Parent->>Parent: "Re-throw error"
    else Timeout occurs
        Parent->>Temporal: "getExternalWorkflowHandle().cancel()"
        Parent->>Activity: "recordTraceEventActivity(NODE_FAILED)"
        Parent->>Parent: "Throw TimeoutError"
    end
```

The parent workflow uses `startChild()` to spawn a new workflow execution, passing the prepared run payload with incremented depth and updated call chain. It then races the child's result against a timeout using `sleep()`.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:476-644]()

### Timeout Handling

Sub-workflows support configurable timeouts:

```typescript
const timeoutSecondsRaw = mergedParams.timeoutSeconds;
const timeoutSeconds = typeof timeoutSecondsRaw === 'number' && Number.isFinite(timeoutSecondsRaw) && timeoutSecondsRaw > 0
  ? Math.floor(timeoutSecondsRaw)
  : 300; // Default: 5 minutes

const timeoutMs = timeoutSeconds * 1000;
const outcome = await Promise.race([
  child.result().then((result) => ({ kind: 'result' as const, result })),
  sleep(timeoutMs).then(() => ({ kind: 'timeout' as const })),
]);

if (outcome.kind === 'timeout') {
  const externalHandle = getExternalWorkflowHandle(child.workflowId);
  await externalHandle.cancel();
  throw ApplicationFailure.nonRetryable(`Sub-workflow timed out after ${timeoutSeconds}s`, 'TimeoutError');
}
```

When a timeout occurs, the parent cancels the child using an external handle and throws a non-retryable failure.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:424-594]()

---

## Retry Policies and Error Handling

### Component Retry Configuration

Components can specify custom retry policies in their metadata:

```typescript
function mapRetryPolicy(policy?: ComponentRetryPolicy) {
  if (!policy) return undefined;

  return {
    maximumAttempts: policy.maxAttempts,
    initialInterval: policy.initialIntervalSeconds ? policy.initialIntervalSeconds * 1000 : undefined,
    maximumInterval: policy.maximumIntervalSeconds ? policy.maximumIntervalSeconds * 1000 : undefined,
    backoffCoefficient: policy.backoffCoefficient,
    nonRetryableErrorTypes: policy.nonRetryableErrorTypes,
  };
}

const retryOptions = mapRetryPolicy(action.retryPolicy);
const { runComponentActivity: runComponentWithRetry } = proxyActivities<{
  runComponentActivity(input: RunComponentActivityInput): Promise<RunComponentActivityOutput>;
}>({
  startToCloseTimeout: '10 minutes',
  retry: retryOptions,
});
```

This converts Component SDK retry policies to Temporal's retry format, enabling automatic retries with exponential backoff.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:140-154](), [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:676-795]()

### Error Categories

| Error Type | Retryable | Description |
|------------|-----------|-------------|
| `NotFoundError` | No | Action not found in actionsByRef map |
| `ValidationError` | No | Missing required inputs or invalid parameters |
| `SubWorkflowDepthError` | No | Maximum nesting depth exceeded |
| `SubWorkflowCycleError` | No | Circular workflow call detected |
| `TimeoutError` | No | Human input or sub-workflow timeout |
| `ToolModeOnly` | No | MCP server component used outside tool mode |
| `ToolsNotReady` | No | Tool dependencies not ready after max wait time |

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:318-840]()

---

## Temporal Integration

### Backend Service Dispatch

The backend's `TemporalService` starts workflow executions:

```typescript
async startWorkflow(options: StartWorkflowOptions): Promise<WorkflowStartResult> {
  const client = await this.getClient();
  const workflowId = options.workflowId ?? `shipsec-workflow-${randomUUID()}`;
  const taskQueue = options.taskQueue ?? this.defaultTaskQueue;

  const workflowFn = this.getWorkflowFunction(options.workflowType);

  const handle = await client.start(workflowFn, {
    workflowId,
    taskQueue,
    args: options.args ?? [],
    memo: options.memo,
    searchAttributes: options.searchAttributes,
  });

  return {
    workflowId: handle.workflowId,
    runId: handle.firstExecutionRunId,
    taskQueue,
  };
}
```

The service maps workflow type strings (e.g., `"shipsecWorkflowRun"`) to workflow function references for type-safe dispatch.

**Sources:** [backend/src/temporal/temporal.service.ts:115-145](), [backend/src/temporal/temporal.service.ts:147-164]()

### Workflow Signals and Queries

The backend can signal running workflows:

```typescript
async signalWorkflow(input: { workflowId: string; signalName: string; args: any }): Promise<void> {
  const handle = await this.getWorkflowHandle({ workflowId: input.workflowId });
  await handle.signal(input.signalName, input.args);
}

async queryWorkflow<T = unknown>(input: { workflowId: string; queryType: string; args?: unknown[] }): Promise<T> {
  const handle = await this.getWorkflowHandle({ workflowId: input.workflowId });
  return handle.query(input.queryType, ...(input.args ?? []));
}
```

These methods are used by the MCP Gateway to signal tool calls and poll for results, and by the human input API to resolve approval requests.

**Sources:** [backend/src/temporal/temporal.service.ts:201-224]()

---

## Worker Architecture

### Worker Initialization

The Temporal worker connects to the server and registers activities:

```typescript
const worker = await Worker.create({
  connection,
  namespace,
  taskQueue,
  workflowsPath,
  activities: {
    runComponentActivity,
    setRunMetadataActivity,
    finalizeRunActivity,
    prepareRunPayloadActivity,
    createHumanInputRequestActivity,
    expireHumanInputRequestActivity,
    recordTraceEventActivity,
    registerComponentToolActivity,
    registerLocalMcpActivity,
    cleanupRunResourcesActivity,
    prepareAndRegisterToolActivity,
    areAllToolsReadyActivity,
    // ... additional activities
  },
  maxConcurrentWorkflowTaskExecutions: 10,
  maxConcurrentActivityTaskExecutions: 10,
});

await worker.run();
```

The worker polls the task queue continuously, executing workflow tasks and activity tasks in separate thread pools.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workers/dev.worker.ts:271-400]()

### Service Adapter Initialization

The worker initializes service adapters that activities use to interact with infrastructure:

```typescript
initializeComponentActivityServices({
  storage: storageAdapter,         // FileStorageAdapter (MinIO)
  trace: traceAdapter,             // KafkaTraceAdapter
  nodeIO: nodeIOAdapter,           // KafkaNodeIOAdapter
  logs: logAdapter,                // KafkaLogAdapter
  secrets: secretsAdapter,         // SecretsAdapter (encrypted DB)
  artifacts: artifactAdapter.factory(),
  terminalStream,                  // RedisTerminalStreamAdapter
  agentTracePublisher,            // KafkaAgentTracePublisher
});
```

These adapters are injected into the execution context of each component, providing abstract interfaces for storage, logging, tracing, and secrets management.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workers/dev.worker.ts:134-232]()

### Namespace and Task Queue Configuration

| Configuration | Environment Variable | Default |
|---------------|---------------------|---------|
| Temporal Address | `TEMPORAL_ADDRESS` | `localhost:7233` |
| Namespace | `TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE` | `shipsec-dev` |
| Task Queue | `TEMPORAL_TASK_QUEUE` | `shipsec-default` |
| Database URL | `DATABASE_URL` | (required) |
| MinIO Endpoint | `MINIO_ENDPOINT` | `localhost` |
| Kafka Brokers | `LOG_KAFKA_BROKERS` | (required) |
| Redis Terminal URL | `TERMINAL_REDIS_URL` | (optional) |

Multi-instance support uses instance-specific namespaces and task queues to isolate execution environments on the same Temporal cluster.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workers/dev.worker.ts:61-92](), [backend/src/temporal/temporal.service.ts:98-104]()

---

## Tool Mode Execution

### Tool Registration Flow

Components in tool mode skip normal execution and register themselves as callable tools:

```typescript
const isToolMode = nodeMetadata?.mode === 'tool';
const isMcpGroup = isMcpGroupComponent(action.componentId);
const shouldSkipExecution = isToolMode && !isMcpGroup;

if (shouldSkipExecution) {
  console.log(`[Workflow] Node ${action.ref} is in tool mode, registering...`);

  if (isMcpServerComponent(action.componentId)) {
    // Start MCP container
    const mcpOutput = await runMcp(activityInput);
    const endpoint = output.endpoint;
    const containerId = output.containerId;

    await registerLocalMcpActivity({
      runId: input.runId,
      nodeId: action.ref,
      toolName: mcpMeta.toolName(mergedParams),
      endpoint,
      containerId,
    });
  } else {
    // Register regular component as tool
    await prepareAndRegisterToolActivity({
      runId: input.runId,
      nodeId: action.ref,
      componentId: action.componentId,
      inputs: mergedInputs,
      params: mergedParams,
    });
  }

  results.set(action.ref, { mode: 'tool', status: 'ready', tools: [] });
  return { activePorts: ['default', 'tools'] };
}
```

MCP groups require special handling: they execute normally to discover child servers, then register the parent as ready after discovery completes.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:678-779]()

### Tool Dependency Waiting

Components with `connectedToolNodeIds` metadata wait for tools to be ready:

```typescript
if (nodeMetadata?.connectedToolNodeIds && nodeMetadata.connectedToolNodeIds.length > 0) {
  const MAX_WAIT_TIME_MS = 120000; // 2 minutes
  const POLL_INTERVAL_MS = 2000;
  const startTime = Date.now();

  while (Date.now() - startTime < MAX_WAIT_TIME_MS) {
    const readyCheck = await areAllToolsReadyActivity({
      runId: input.runId,
      requiredNodeIds: nodeMetadata.connectedToolNodeIds,
    });

    if (readyCheck.ready) {
      break;
    }

    await sleep(POLL_INTERVAL_MS);
  }

  const finalReadyCheck = await areAllToolsReadyActivity({
    runId: input.runId,
    requiredNodeIds: nodeMetadata.connectedToolNodeIds,
  });

  if (!finalReadyCheck.ready) {
    throw ApplicationFailure.nonRetryable(
      `Tools not ready after ${MAX_WAIT_TIME_MS}ms: ${nodeMetadata.connectedToolNodeIds.join(', ')}`,
      'ToolsNotReady'
    );
  }
}
```

This ensures AI agents don't start before their required tools are registered and available.

**Sources:** [worker/src/temporal/workflows/index.ts:798-840]()

---

## Schedule-Triggered Execution

### Schedule-to-Workflow Bridge

Temporal schedules trigger the `scheduleTriggerWorkflow`, which prepares and dispatches the actual workflow:

```typescript
export interface ScheduleTriggerWorkflowArgs {
  workflowId: string;
  workflowVersionId?: string | null;
  workflowVersion?: number | null;
  organizationId?: string | null;
  scheduleId?: string;
  scheduleName?: string | null;
  runtimeInputs?: Record<string, unknown>;
  nodeOverrides?: Record<string, { params?: Record<string, unknown>; inputOverrides?: Record<string, unknown> }>;
  trigger?: ExecutionTriggerMetadata;
}
```

The schedule service builds dispatch args with embedded trigger metadata:

```typescript
const dispatchArgs = this.buildDispatchArgs({
  workflowId: record.workflowId,
  workflowVersionId: record.workflowVersionId,
  workflowVersion: record.workflowVersion,
  organizationId: record.organizationId ?? null,
  scheduleId: record.id,
  scheduleName: record.name,
  payload: normalizedPayload,
});

await this.temporalService.createSchedule({
  scheduleId: record.id,
  organizationId: context.organizationId,
  cronExpression: record.cronExpression,
  timezone: record.timezone,
  overlapPolicy: record.overlapPolicy ?? 'skip',
  dispatchArgs,
});
```

**Sources:** [backend/src/temporal/temporal.service.ts:73-86](), [backend/src/schedules/schedules.service.ts:72-106]()

### Overlap Policies

Schedules support three overlap policies to control concurrent executions:

| Policy | Temporal Constant | Behavior |
|--------|------------------|----------|
| `skip` | `SKIP` | Skip new run if previous still running |
| `buffer` | `BUFFER_ONE` | Queue one new run to start after current finishes |
