## Entry Point: Primary Use Case

The Entry Point component demonstrates the most common dynamic port pattern: converting parameter-defined runtime inputs into output ports.

### Entry Point Configuration

Users define runtime inputs via the `runtimeInputs` parameter:

```json
{
  "runtimeInputs": [
    {
      "id": "targetUrl",
      "label": "Target URL",
      "type": "text",
      "required": true,
      "description": "URL to scan"
    },
    {
      "id": "depth",
      "label": "Scan Depth",
      "type": "number",
      "required": false,
      "defaultValue": 3
    }
  ]
}
```

### Dynamic Output Generation

The Entry Point's `resolvePorts()` function converts each runtime input into a corresponding output port:

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Parameter Configuration"
        P["runtimeInputs parameter"]
    end

    subgraph "Dynamic Port Resolution"
        R["resolvePorts() function"]
    end

    subgraph "Generated Outputs"
        O1["targetUrl output<br/>(connectionType: text)"]
        O2["depth output<br/>(connectionType: number)"]
        O3["__runtimeData output<br/>(connectionType: json)"]
    end

    P --> R
    R --> O1
    R --> O2
    R --> O3
```

The Entry Point always generates:
- One output per runtime input definition (matching the input's `type` field to a `connectionType`)
- A special `__runtimeData` output containing all runtime inputs as a JSON object

