## Dual-Store Architecture

The pipeline uses a **dual-store pattern** to separate raw data collection from visual state computation:

**Dual-Store Responsibilities**

```mermaid
graph TB
    SSE["SSE Events<br/>(raw streams)"]

    ExecStore["useExecutionStore"]
    ExecOps["• mergeById() deduplication<br/>• Raw events[] storage<br/>• Simple deriveNodeStates()<br/>• SSE connection lifecycle"]

    TimelineStore["useExecutionTimelineStore"]
    TimelineOps["• prepareTimelineEvents()<br/>• normalizeDataPackets()<br/>• calculateNodeStates()<br/>• Playback control"]

    UI["UI Components<br/>ExecutionInspector<br/>ExecutionTimeline<br/>EventInspector"]

    SSE --> ExecStore
    ExecStore --> ExecOps
    ExecOps -.Zustand Subscribe.-> TimelineStore
    TimelineStore --> TimelineOps
    TimelineOps --> UI

    style ExecOps fill:#f9f9f9
    style TimelineOps fill:#f9f9f9
```

**Why two stores?**

1. **Separation of concerns**: Raw data aggregation (SSE, polling, dedup) is isolated from visual calculations (progress, timeline positions)
2. **Performance**: Visual state recalculations only trigger when `currentTime` or `events` change, not on every SSE event
3. **Testability**: Visual state logic can be tested independently from network concerns
4. **Reusability**: `useExecutionStore` serves canvas badges, `useExecutionTimelineStore` serves timeline UI

**Store Communication:**

```typescript
// useExecutionTimelineStore subscribes to useExecutionStore
export const initializeTimelineStore = () => {
  unsubscribeExecutionStore = useExecutionStore.subscribe((state) => {
    const { events: liveEvents, runId } = state;
    const timelineStore = useExecutionTimelineStore.getState();

    // When new events arrive, timeline store transforms them
    if (timelineStore.playbackMode === 'live' && liveEvents.length > 0) {
      timelineStore.updateFromLiveEvent(liveEvents[liveEvents.length - 1]);
    }
  });
};
```

