## Performance Optimizations

### Event Batching

SSE events arrive in batches to reduce network overhead. The backend accumulates events over a short window (typically 100-500ms) before emitting:

```typescript
// Backend batches events per SSE emit
event: trace
data: {"events": [...], "cursor": "xyz"}

// Frontend merges entire batch at once
const mergedEvents = mergeEvents(state.events, payload.events);
```

### Selective Re-renders

The dual-store pattern minimizes UI re-renders:

- `useExecutionStore` updates trigger only when new raw data arrives
- `useExecutionTimelineStore` updates trigger only when visual state changes (playback position, node states)
- Components use granular selectors to avoid unnecessary renders

Example selector usage:

```typescript
// Only re-render when selectedRunId changes
const selectedRunId = useExecutionTimelineStore(state => state.selectedRunId);

// Only re-render when specific node state changes
const nodeState = useExecutionTimelineStore(
  state => state.nodeStates[nodeId]
);
```

### Cursor-based Resumption

Both SSE and HTTP endpoints support cursor-based resumption to avoid re-fetching already processed events:

```typescript
// Cursors persist across reconnection
const { cursor, terminalCursor, logCursor } = get();
const source = await api.executions.stream(runId, {
  cursor,           // Resume trace events
  terminalCursor,   // Resume terminal chunks
  logCursor,        // Resume logs
});
```

Sources: [frontend/src/store/executionStore.ts:388-404]()

